invariant: INV-50
file: sha512.c
catches: sha2_equiv_test
reason: a host object's finalize writes zero as the upper word of the
  length where FIPS 180-4 §5.1.2 writes the upper 64 bits of the bit
  count, which are the top three bits of the byte count. Every message
  under 2^61 bytes has the same digest, and no message a test hashes is
  that long, so the published vectors and the Wycheproof suites pass.
  sha512_hw.c compiles the same text for x86-64. bin/sha2_equiv_test
  starts a context at a byte count of 2^61 - 1 and at larger ones,
  absorbs a few bytes and compares the digest with one whose padding the
  test writes from a 128-bit product (test/sha2_equiv_length512.h).

--- old
// big-endian integer, written into the pending block as the second arm's finalize writes it.
static void finalize(sha512 *s) {
    uint64_t high = s->total_bytes >> 61;
--- new
// big-endian integer, written into the pending block as the second arm's finalize writes it.
static void finalize(sha512 *s) {
    uint64_t high = 0;
